==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|desjoindre|lang=fro}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/dɪsˈdʒɔɪnt/}}
* {{rhymes|ɔɪnt}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# not smooth or [[continuous]]; [[disjointed]]
# {{context|set theory|lang=en}} (''not used in the comparative or superlative'') Of two or more [[set]]s, having no members in common; having an [[intersection]] equal to the [[empty set]].

====Antonyms====
* [[non-disjoint]]
* [[overlapping]]

====Translations====
{{trans-top|having no members in common}}
* Chinese:
*: {{ttbc|cmn}}: {{t|cmn|不相交的|sc=Hani}}
* Czech: {{t-|cs|disjunktní|m}}
* German: {{t+|de|disjunkt}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Icelandic: {{t-|is|sundurlægur|m}}, {{t-|is|óskaraður|m}}
* Korean: {{t-|ko|서로소|tr=seoroso|sc=Kore}}
* Portuguese: {{t-|pt|disjunto}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Related terms====
* [[disjunct]]
* [[conjoint]]

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To render [[disjoint#Adjective|disjoint]]; to remove a connection, linkage, or intersection.
#: ''to '''disjoint''' limbs; to '''disjoint''' bones; to '''disjoint''' poultry by carving''
#* Prior
#*: Yet what could swords or poisons, racks or flame, / But mangle and '''disjoint''' the brittle frame?
#* Longfellow
#*: Some half-ruined wall / '''Disjointed''' and about to fall.
# To break the natural order and relations of; to make incoherent.
#: ''a '''disjointed''' speech''
# To fall into pieces.
#: {{rfquotek|Shakespeare}}

====Translations====
